                    <div class="question">
                      <div><strong>Q1.</strong> Which of the following statements is true regarding inheritance in Python?
                        <span style="font-size: 0.9em; color: #555;">(25 marks)</span>
                      </div>
                      <div class="options">
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>A.</strong> Python supports multiple inheritance.
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>B.</strong> All Python classes must inherit from <code style="background-color: #f5f5f5; padding: 2px 4px; border-radius: 3px; font-family: monospace;">object</code> explicitly.
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>C.</strong> Python only supports single inheritance.
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>D.</strong> Inheritance cannot be used to override methods.
                          </div>
                        
                      </div>
                      <div class="bubble-row">
                        <strong>Q1:</strong>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>A</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>B</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>C</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>D</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  
                    <div class="question">
                      <div><strong>Q2.</strong> What is the primary purpose of using decorators in Python?
                        <span style="font-size: 0.9em; color: #555;">(25 marks)</span>
                      </div>
                      <div class="options">
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>A.</strong> To define class attributes.
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>B.</strong> To implement recursion in functions.
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>C.</strong> To decorate the syntax of functions.
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>D.</strong> To modify the functionality of a function or class method.
                          </div>
                        
                      </div>
                      <div class="bubble-row">
                        <strong>Q2:</strong>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>A</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>B</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>C</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>D</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  
                    <div class="question">
                      <div><strong>Q3.</strong> How do you define a constructor in a Python class?
                        <span style="font-size: 0.9em; color: #555;">(25 marks)</span>
                      </div>
                      <div class="options">
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>A.</strong> def construct(self):
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>B.</strong> def __init__(self):
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>C.</strong> def constructor(self):
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>D.</strong> def _construct(self):
                          </div>
                        
                      </div>
                      <div class="bubble-row">
                        <strong>Q3:</strong>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>A</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>B</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>C</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>D</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  
                    <div class="question">
                      <div><strong>Q4.</strong> What is the output of the following code?

<pre style="background-color: #f5f5f5; padding: 10px; border-radius: 4px; font-family: monospace; white-space: pre-wrap;">python
class Example:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value
    def __str__(self):
        return f'Value is {self.value}'

obj = Example(5)
print(obj)
</pre>

                        <span style="font-size: 0.9em; color: #555;">(25 marks)</span>
                      </div>
                      <div class="options">
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>A.</strong> An error is raised.
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>B.</strong> <__main__.Example object at memory_location>
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>C.</strong> None
                          </div>
                        
                          <div class="option">
                            <strong>D.</strong> Value is 5
                          </div>
                        
                      </div>
                      <div class="bubble-row">
                        <strong>Q4:</strong>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>A</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
                        <span style="margin-left: 10px;"><strong>B</strong> <span class="bubble"></span></span>
